body:
I should add, this is my first pull request on Godot, so bear with me if I do anything awkward. I am still finding my way. 

I was trying to keep the explanation short, and also not prematurely assume knowledge levels. However given that this PR is a breaking change for discussion, perhaps it is worth attempting to elaborate (at the risk of trying to educate people who may already know more about this than me):

WHAT IS BIAS AND WHY SHOULDN’T IT BE 0.8

The iterative impulse solver at the heart of Godot Physics is known as a velocity solver, because the only thing it tries to control is the velocity of the objects. It doesn't move them itself, that is done in a seperate integration step. It basically looks at all the collisions and joint constraints and ensures that the right forces (impulses) are applied so that all objects have the correct velocities at the end of the solve. Those ‘correct’ velocities are ones that don’t cause further interpenetration, or cause joints to breach their locks or limits.

The constraints are things like: “BodyA is in contact with BodyB, so zero out any velocity moving them closer along their contact normal”, or “The PinJoint2D linear velocity should always be zero”. 

So a velocity solver fixes velocity errors, but joints and contacts can also develop position errors due to drift and limitations of the engine. The solver will not do anything about position errors without adding an additional technique. The classic (Baumgarte) technique resolves positional error by measuring the positional error each frame for each constraint and then adding a bit of extra velocity to your objects in order to try to close up that gap. 

An example / analogy may help: Imagine you are playing asteroids and your ship is 1m away from where it should be due to some other physics error. The bias technique solves this error by adding a velocity to your spaceship of:

bias_velocity = bias * distance error / time_delta

So a bias of 1.0 would add so much velocity to your spaceship that you hit your target position in the next 1/60th of a second. However on the next frame, you are at your target, but travelling at 60m/s, so the next frame after that will see you 1m away again and you will oscillate. So you don’t use a bias of 1.0, or 0.8, because you just end up overshooting and oscillating (or bouncing too hard). While you can eliminate that overshoot by just stopping when you get there, think about how that looks to the user, and how that teleport might affect the system if the movement then forced your spaceship into another object, creating a new (and possibly more problematic) positional error.

Another way of looking at it, you have injected 1/2 m * v ^2 energy into the system with your bias velocity. If you had injected 1/4 of that velocity, your energy would have been 1/16th of what it was (at least on the next frame) and you wouldn’t overshoot. Injecting unnecessary energy into a system is exactly why my horde of enemies look like a boiling fluid (when bias is 0.8) rather than a wave (when bias is 0.2). It’s just heat / chaos, and it is beyond the capability of the physics rate to properly sample it. At a bias of 0.2, my enemies resolve their differences more calmly by not jumping to extremes and by flexing just a little.

Bias is a kind of hack number because a different bias is needed for different mass combinations or time deltas, but the recommendation I recall is somewhere between 0.1 and 0.3 is good. Modern physics engines often use a parameterisation that replaces bias with something that models a physical spring and damper. This provides more assured and accurate behaviour that adjusts for different mass distributions and time deltas.

High bias is not always bad, but I think it is usually bad when you make it high by default for all your bodies. It can be tweaked for different bodies and joints to help create a priority system for which entities are the most unyielding. The high frequency response can create sharper animations in isolated cases or small systems.

GODOT SPECIFICALLY

Godot is actually a split impulse engine which records all the extra bias velocities separately, and ensures that those extra velocities can be removed or damped out on the next frame. That’s great, it addresses the crude overshooting spaceship I described above, however I don’t think that means you can just up the bias, because you are still modelling a system that should move and respond to issues smoothly. It should not respond in a high frequency way that exceeds the physics sampling capability and injects excess energy.

Even though you remove the velocity you added on the next frame, that doesn’t help you if you inadvertently set up a sort of bias oscillation, or you just pass the energy on to whatever you ran in to (and we are talking about 16 times the energy with a bias of 0.8 versus a bias of 0.2). 

There is one other non-physical thing that Godot does with the application of the bias velocity, which is to artificially inject more bias into the linear velocity, rather than via the contact point. This looks like a non-physical change to help pass box stacking and prevent jitter in the presence of too much bias...but I don't know.

BIAS CALCULATION EXCERPTS FROM GODOT :

```
case PhysicsServer2D::SPACE_PARAM_CONTACT_DEFAULT_BIAS:
	contact_bias = p_value;
	break;

...

real_t max_penetration = space->get_contact_max_allowed_penetration();

real_t bias = space->get_contact_bias();

GodotShape2D *shape_A_ptr = A->get_shape(shape_A);
GodotShape2D *shape_B_ptr = B->get_shape(shape_B);

if (shape_A_ptr->get_custom_bias() || shape_B_ptr->get_custom_bias()) {
	if (shape_A_ptr->get_custom_bias() == 0) {
		bias = shape_B_ptr->get_custom_bias();
	} else if (shape_B_ptr->get_custom_bias() == 0) {
		bias = shape_A_ptr->get_custom_bias();
	} else {
		bias = (shape_B_ptr->get_custom_bias() + shape_A_ptr->get_custom_bias()) * 0.5;
	}
}

...

c.bias = -bias * inv_dt * MIN(0.0f, -depth + max_penetration);

…

real_t jbn = (c.bias - vbn) * c.mass_normal;
```

WHAT IF I AM WRONG?

It is always possible that Godot uniquely wants a larger bias for some reason that I can’t unpack yet, or that my education and experience is lacking. I can't describe everything Godot is doing at the moment, or the implications. I haven't spent enough time with it.

So if my thinking is flawed here, or I have missed something, hopefully someone can pick that up. But from what understanding and experience I have, this should probably be fixed. It seems like a glaring mistake to me.

I am also hoping that someone might remember if this was once set to 0.2, or maybe why it was changed (if it was changed). Or maybe where it was set before the 2023 change that put it in the physics server, so I can track it further back.

I just did box stacking and pyramid tests again and they seem to fail at similar solver iteration counts whether bias is at 0.8 or 0.2.

3D PHYSICS

Also, it looks like it is 0.8 in physics_server_3d as well. I see no reason that it wouldn’t also need to be 0.2, but I haven’t been testing 3D myself much or looked into what the engine does with it, so I am feeling far less assured of that.

As I would expect, Jolt doesn’t use this bias parameter. 
```
case PhysicsServer3D::SPACE_PARAM_CONTACT_DEFAULT_BIAS: {
	WARN_PRINT("Space-specific contact default bias is not supported when using Jolt Physics. Any such value will be ignored.");
} break;
```
